Storage & Databases on AWS: Everything You Must Know (High-Level Overview)

AWS provides a rich ecosystem of storage and database services — each designed for specific performance, durability, and scalability needs.

Understanding which service to choose and when is one of the most important cloud architecture skills and a high-frequency interview topic.

Here’s a crisp, practical breakdown



 1️⃣ Storage Services on AWS

📦 Amazon S3 (Object Storage)

Store any amount of data

Durable (11 9’s), scalable, low cost

Supports versioning, lifecycle rules, storage classes
📍 Use Case: Backups, logs, static websites, data lakes, media storage



💾 Amazon EBS (Block Storage)

Block-level volumes for EC2

High performance, low latency
📍 Use Case: Databases, boot volumes, transactional apps



📁 Amazon EFS (File Storage)

NFS-based shared file system

Auto-scaling across multiple AZs
📍 Use Case: Shared apps, CMS systems, microservices



🧊 Amazon Glacier (Archive Storage)

Extremely low-cost archival

Retrieval from minutes → hours
📍 Use Case: Long-term backups, compliance archives



2️⃣ Database Services on AWS

🗄️ Amazon RDS (Relational Database Service)

Fully managed MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server, MariaDB

Multi-AZ, automated backups, read replicas
📍 Use Case: Traditional relational workloads



Amazon Aurora

Cloud-native RDS engine (MySQL & PostgreSQL compatible)

5× faster than MySQL, 3× faster than PostgreSQL
📍 Use Case: High-performance enterprise apps



⚡ Amazon DynamoDB (NoSQL)

Serverless, highly scalable key-value/document store

Single-digit millisecond latency
📍 Use Case: Gaming, IoT, high-scale apps, e-commerce carts



📊 Amazon Redshift (Data Warehouse)

Petabyte-scale analytics
📍 Use Case: BI/Analytics, big data workloads



🧠 Amazon Neptune (Graph Database)

📍 Use Case: Social graphs, fraud detection, recommendation engines



🧩 Amazon ElastiCache (Redis/Memcached)

📍 Use Case: Caching, session management, real-time apps



